  impossible goal if the requirement is  for people to own a game console but you  can reach an audience that is factors  bigger than the current market of  console owners by making games available  where the audience is and that's  everywhere  Xbox game pass allows for this  especially with X cloud scheduled to be  integrated into the service later this  year once X cloud becomes folded into  game pass Microsoft will edge closer to  creating the elusive and potentially  lucrative Netflix of games currently  Xbox game past subscribers must download  their games and with file sizes for some  larger games stretching beyond a hundred  gigabytes and possibly growing even  larger in the next generation this can  be a long and laborious process  integrating X cloud will let you stream  a service already available on console  mobile phones and PC increasing the  addressable market for Xbox Microsoft  still wants to sell you a console and  for the next 10 years or so that will  most likely remain the best and most  reliable way to play games it may always  be the preferred way for some to play  games but Microsoft is preparing for a  future where it's not the only way the  key metric to success for Xbox in this  new world is your xbox live subscription  and not necessarily the number of  consoles sold an Xbox Live Gold user is  either paying Microsoft ten dollars a  month or $60 a year which is the kind of  steady recurring revenue that any  business wants to see every person on  earth who owns a connected device a  phone a tablet a PC or a console is a  potential Xbox Live user Spencer's new  vision for Xbox is making a run at  bringing you into the fold an Xbox game  pass is central to that goal Xbox game  pass is only worth it if Microsoft has a  steady flow of games to support it and  to that end Microsoft is bigger than  it's ever been in terms of its game  development footprint the company now  operates 15 internal studios as part of  its Xbox game studios label all of which  are working on next-gen projects if  Microsoft can successfully line up  production pipelines and schedule  releases from its internal teams  not to mention third parties it could  create a Netflix type scenario where  there is always something new on the  horizon so far Microsoft has announced  that halo Infinite will release on Xbox  game paths as well as all future gears  Forza fable games obsidians new title  grounded ninja theories hell-blade  sequel and rares new IP ever wild  Microsoft has no doubt spent a pretty  penny to buy these studios and invest in  their projects but you got to spend  money to make money  perhaps the most exciting part about  Xbox game paths is  could lead to new and creative games  from teams we might not have otherwise  heard about like Netflix which is green  lighting and funding the kinds of TV  shows and movies that traditional  studios might not take on Xbox game  passes seemingly setting up something  similar a developer can launch their  game into Xbox game paths with the  understanding that the built-in audience  from day one is already million-strong  even if you're keen for Microsoft's  vision for the future of gaming there  are still some lingering questions I  wonder if subscription services like  Xbox game paths will end up devaluing  games in the long run and if so if  that's a problem for Microsoft or  developers to bear the latest GDC state  of the industry survey showed that more  than a quarter of surveyed developers  believe a subscription model erodes the  value of a game devolver digital  co-founder Graham Struthers tells  GameSpot the world of subscription is a  worry you do wonder if it's going to  lead to a situation where there is so  much content that you kind of fall off  the edge that's the one that keeps us up  at night there is also the concern of  further ceding ownership of your games  to a monolithic company that can change  its policies whenever it pleases to  achieve whatever goals it has that have  nothing to do with you  all in all there has never been a game  subscription program as ambitious as  Xbox game pass and if it gains traction  it could very well change the way you  play games would Microsoft have pursued  such an ambitious strategy have the  roles been reversed and Xbox came out on  top in the last generation we can never  know but one thing's for sure this feels  like a watershed moment in gaming and  that's really exciting at the start of a  new console generation  you  [Music]
 

